questions.explanation:
Shortcut Trick
For any triangle, the angle subtended by the incenter at any two vertices is 90&deg; plus half of the third angle.

&rArr; &ang;BIC = 90&deg; + (&ang;BAC / 2)

&rArr; 125&deg; = 90&deg; + (&ang;BAC / 2)

&rArr; &ang;BAC / 2 = 125&deg; &minus; 90&deg;

&rArr; &ang;BAC = 35&deg; × 2 = 70&deg;

**&there4; The correct answer is 70&deg;.**

**Alternate Method**

**Given:**

The angle bisectors of &ang;ABC and &ang;ACB meet at the incenter I.

Measure of &ang;BIC = 125&deg;

**Formula Used:**

Sum of interior angles of a triangle = 180&deg;

An angle bisector divides an angle into two equal parts.





  A
  B
  C
  I
  125&deg;
  ? (70&deg;)

**Calculations:**

In &Delta;BIC, the sum of angles is 180&deg;.

&rArr; &ang;IBC + &ang;ICB + &ang;BIC = 180&deg;

Since BI and CI are the angle bisectors of &ang;ABC and &ang;ACB respectively:

&rArr; &ang;IBC = &ang;ABC / 2

&rArr; &ang;ICB = &ang;ACB / 2

Substituting these into the equation for &Delta;BIC:

&rArr; (&ang;ABC / 2) + (&ang;ACB / 2) + 125&deg; = 180&deg;

&rArr; (&ang;ABC + &ang;ACB) / 2 = 180&deg; &minus; 125&deg;

&rArr; (&ang;ABC + &ang;ACB) / 2 = 55&deg;

&rArr; &ang;ABC + &ang;ACB = 55&deg; × 2 = 110&deg;

Now, in &Delta;ABC, the sum of all interior angles is 180&deg;.

&rArr; &ang;BAC + &ang;ABC + &ang;ACB = 180&deg;

&rArr; &ang;BAC + 110&deg; = 180&deg;

&rArr; &ang;BAC = 180&deg; &minus; 110&deg;

&rArr; &ang;BAC = 70&deg;

**&there4; The correct answer is 70&deg;.**

**Additional Information**

Incenter

The incenter is the point where all three internal angle bisectors of a triangle intersect. The central angle formed by two bisectors is 90&deg; + (&ang;Opposite Vertex) / 2.

Circumcenter

The intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides. The angle subtended by an arc at the circumcenter is double the angle subtended at any point on the circle (&ang;BOC = 2 × &ang;A).

Orthocenter

The point where the altitudes of the triangle meet. The sum of the angle at the orthocenter and the opposite vertex angle is always 180&deg; (&ang;BHC + &ang;A = 180&deg;).

Excenter

The intersection of the external bisectors of two angles and the internal bisector of the third angle. The angle formed by the external bisectors is 90&deg; &minus; (&ang;Opposite Vertex) / 2.

